              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-slate-700">
                {
                  circuitRows.map((row, index) => (
                    <tr class={index === circuitRows.length - 1 ? "" : "border-b border-slate-100"}>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold text-slate-900">{row.amps} A · {row.volts} V</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-4">{row.continuous ? "Continuous" : "Non-continuous"}</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-4">{fmtInt(row.circuitVa)} VA</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-4">{fmtInt(row.usable)} VA</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">{row.count}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
            Sources &amp; standards: NEC (NFPA 70) 2023 — <strong>220.14(I)</strong> 180 VA per receptacle outlet
            on one strap or yoke, <strong>220.14(J)</strong> dwelling receptacles included in the general lighting
            load, <strong>220.12</strong> general lighting unit loads, <strong>210.23(A)(1)</strong> and
            <strong>210.23(A)(2)</strong> connected-load limits, <strong>210.11(C)(1)–(3)</strong> required
            dedicated dwelling circuits, <strong>210.52</strong> receptacle placement, <strong>210.19(A)</strong>
            and <strong>210.20(A)</strong> continuous load. Some jurisdictions impose an outlet-count limit by
            local amendment even though the model code does not; local amendments override and the AHJ has final
            say.
          </p>
